#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133396 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90adf237-9627-55f1-8f90-8fbc4acf02fc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "2d4b2c66-5600-5712-8ec9-3d7325b247c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11348" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 4x^3  \\: \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the line $z = 4-y$ in the first quadrant." :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 4x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}V\n= \n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}} 4x^3 \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}4{x}^{3}\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}[{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{(\\sqrt{y})}^{4}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/3c8712fa0a30ad94f503efe5e080ee89f83eb21f19fba45033bbedeab08ac816.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{y}^{2}z]_{z=0}^{z=4-y}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{y}^{2}(4-y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}4{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(64-0)-\\frac{1}{4}(256-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133397 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd48398-cb9d-579f-b7f7-0f6be19172ce" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "295afdb2-2e90-5f1a-8830-d3886d99fbad" :question/math-academy-id "e-11349" :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{0}^{z} \\dfrac{\\cos (2y)}{1-y} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (y,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2\\sqrt{1-y} \\right\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}[x]_{x=0}^{x=z}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}(z-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-y}}\\frac{z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}{[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2\\sqrt{1-y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}(\\frac{4(1-y)}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133403 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e612d4ba-b772-5f56-a031-fdfc05d63221" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region Given a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "ad3e88cb-f213-509b-93c5-3fc529d617c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12369" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/fc19dd4415663ab027e0fc386d4c5bc81a28c1bc26d5f6cf2083f4f4228a4dea.png)\n\n \n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/f25c393f10c35ffd9b4fa91cdf672a516cf6cff974a8aa563d350619a64b79bd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y-z \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/145f0d494ffe24de84e3a289b3a7316d3f4933f193d734aed118609f7fb9f94f.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iiint \\limits_{R}  12z^2  \\: \\textrm{d}V \n= \n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{3-y-z}  12z^2  \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-y-z}12{z}^{2}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-y-z}12{z}^{2}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{z}^{2}[x]_{x=0}^{x=3-y-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{z}^{2}(3-y-z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{z}^{2}-12y{z}^{2}-12{z}^{3})\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{z}^{2}-12y{z}^{2}-12{z}^{3})\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{z}^{2}-12y{z}^{2}-12{z}^{3})\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[12{z}^{3}-4y{z}^{3}-3{z}^{4}]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12-4y-3)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9-4y)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9-4y)\\,dy & =[9y-2{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(9-2)-0 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a22ed84a-4b6f-53b3-8336-bf592aed8686" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "b51667a8-f48a-59a2-b09e-59c6fcad5507" :question/math-academy-id "e-12398" :question/problem "Find the missing limits in the repeated integral below\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V\n= \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \nf(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type II region defined as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -y^2 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{y}, \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq ye^{z+2} \\right\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: u_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq u_2(y,z) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/1b49704bf6d5837a18d8b5dbb8b420d430d5e3881507540fb62a45cf1f6ce52f.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\int_{u_1(y,z)}^{u_2(y,z)} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm{d}x \\right]\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu_1(y,z) = -2, \\qquad u_2(y,z)  = ye^{z+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V \n= \n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{-2}^{ye^{z+2}} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD =\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -y^2 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2}^{y{e}^{z+2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{-2}^{y{e}^{z+2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dz\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{-2}^{y{e}^{z+2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a22ed84a-4b6f-53b3-8336-bf592aed8686" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffaaf56c-f7f4-57da-806d-dc61326cb39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{yz}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345773} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7359d7-c41c-510d-9c53-b2694b769ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{yz}^{4}[{∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,dA]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345774} {:answer/id #uuid "8507c09c-eabf-5b6d-be58-d771365a0529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{yz}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345775} {:answer/id #uuid "0454310d-f20c-5520-be0b-e0a39b2bbaa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{yz}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345776} {:answer/id #uuid "754468a8-049d-540c-b24b-1964c5fea1bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{yz}^{4}[{∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,dA]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0454310d-f20c-5520-be0b-e0a39b2bbaa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{yz}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345776} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8311714-cfc5-518b-b248-b0613bcb14f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "283fbbb8-94f0-5ba7-a1c7-86d8e2c0f6e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161545" :question/problem "Consider the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,yz\\le x\\le 4},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $yz$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/1b49704bf6d5837a18d8b5dbb8b420d430d5e3881507540fb62a45cf1f6ce52f.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=yz\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{yz}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a22ed84a-4b6f-53b3-8336-bf592aed8686" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7da49eb5-2c4b-5895-b48e-9a2f0dc66a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-3}^{5{y}^{4}+2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345780} {:answer/id #uuid "cd74baa0-144c-5982-8e6f-741425d9f5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-3}^{5{y}^{4}+2}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345781} {:answer/id #uuid "32daf30e-1fd3-5377-9c73-44580b3d79d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{5{y}^{4}+2}[{∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,dA]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345782} {:answer/id #uuid "f91ca9ec-5968-5811-96ee-d3283eddcc14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-3}^{5{y}^{4}+2}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345783} {:answer/id #uuid "e7863a08-121e-54d6-b8fd-c1c927175956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{5{y}^{4}+2}[{∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,dA]dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd74baa0-144c-5982-8e6f-741425d9f5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-3}^{5{y}^{4}+2}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345781} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19b6d60-39d9-557f-a2b7-ff61f1e45fea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "625feabe-c8f9-55d8-bf51-32aafcb00a8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161548" :question/problem "Consider the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,-3\\le x\\le 5{y}^{4}+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $yz$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/1b49704bf6d5837a18d8b5dbb8b420d430d5e3881507540fb62a45cf1f6ce52f.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=-3\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)=5{y}^{4}+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-3}^{5{y}^{4}+2}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd48398-cb9d-579f-b7f7-0f6be19172ce" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83fc4178-3778-561f-a894-30026f919f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345787} {:answer/id #uuid "855588bd-17c6-5cb3-905b-eea06e2c2a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345788} {:answer/id #uuid "99da2280-53f2-5e5b-b72d-02dcf3fa4b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345789} {:answer/id #uuid "e3437526-8df8-5c32-8411-217b35638a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345790} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4f2f4e-5fde-56ef-b4db-a58c33a6f28a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "855588bd-17c6-5cb3-905b-eea06e2c2a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345788} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f29b384-fa33-5b83-9cd5-e308d52b498a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e407a798-2ab7-5ae6-bcb2-5c2001782993" :question/math-academy-id "q-161560" :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y+2z}2\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2y}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y+2z}2\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2[x]_{x=0}^{x=y+2z}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(y+2z-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y+4z\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2y+4z\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}2y+4z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[2yz+2{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2y(2y)+2(2y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}4{y}^{2}+8{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}12{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[4{y}^{3}]_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =4(27-1) \\\\  & =104\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd48398-cb9d-579f-b7f7-0f6be19172ce" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b47d6d3-ab78-5890-a21a-f5fa838567e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345794} {:answer/id #uuid "25e5a23d-8498-5e55-96b2-45a6b80d7cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345795} {:answer/id #uuid "d4458c12-032b-5ed8-bc56-f9489cfd37e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345796} {:answer/id #uuid "30e37ac7-9a9b-565c-9556-318b6faea207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345797} {:answer/id #uuid "d26036a4-f7e8-543d-b97c-ad9a77a319e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b47d6d3-ab78-5890-a21a-f5fa838567e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345794} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b3538a-313c-52d6-b33e-cc222a5aa658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a06af5b6-6503-5e3b-8a72-567c64d6a27c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161561" :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}4xz\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{4-2y}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}4xz\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}[2z{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=z}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2z({z}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2y}}2{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{4-2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{4-2y)})^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}(4-2y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}(16-16y+4{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8-8y+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[8y-4{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =16-16+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a22ed84a-4b6f-53b3-8336-bf592aed8686" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce5d283-2b53-5584-b58e-605c9ec84740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345801} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fc01a3-79f2-52f8-aaf4-4bf5c2cdbf85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345802} {:answer/id #uuid "f1519679-dfcf-5e0e-95f8-2f0071168365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345803} {:answer/id #uuid "069a7d4d-2ef7-5a82-aed4-b61b50f08be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345804} {:answer/id #uuid "c0da0498-6009-583b-a739-a3b823bfe474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1519679-dfcf-5e0e-95f8-2f0071168365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345803} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d9b4dcc-8dfb-5857-bf6a-c65f904912c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bf1bbf2-7733-5ac2-a5df-579136f67cb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161563" :question/problem "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,2y\\le z\\le 8,\\,0\\le x\\le {z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/a905e1f60fb0aa865c071fcbccbfecb30dcac7a74b4b647d24a40f4c89ff1935.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)={z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,2y\\le z\\le 8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{2y}^{8}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dz\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{2y}^{8}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=2y+8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a22ed84a-4b6f-53b3-8336-bf592aed8686" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e59a877-7829-5aa5-b8de-39ab9aec25e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345808} {:answer/id #uuid "2111342a-b606-54b5-a86e-fce6f1046809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\sqrt{y+z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345809} {:answer/id #uuid "80b554f3-044f-5a61-92d5-63dd1178435f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\sqrt{y+z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345810} {:answer/id #uuid "831ffbb3-65a6-581f-be22-8a37de7b8634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345811} {:answer/id #uuid "27b5d1f3-ee0f-56e9-88e7-94ed56436816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e59a877-7829-5aa5-b8de-39ab9aec25e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345808} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf378297-4efc-59da-9476-d79c43e94ceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d1c0425-d74e-54e3-8f7e-dc33ee94df6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161564" :question/problem "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz\n$$\n\nis defined over the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-z\\le y\\le 1,\\,4\\le z\\le 5,\\,-\\sqrt{y+z}\\le x\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/a905e1f60fb0aa865c071fcbccbfecb30dcac7a74b4b647d24a40f4c89ff1935.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=-\\sqrt{y+z},\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-z\\le y\\le 1,\\,4\\le z\\le 5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{4}^{5}{\\int }_{-z}^{1}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dy\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{5}{\\int }_{-z}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=4+5=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a22ed84a-4b6f-53b3-8336-bf592aed8686" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a52495d-c609-55ee-84e8-757277db0d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+2z-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345815} {:answer/id #uuid "561d8166-9d56-5672-8862-d9a312ad1674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡z-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345816} {:answer/id #uuid "373d34ce-61bb-5a3a-a3a6-fef504beeec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345817} {:answer/id #uuid "6433b86e-c036-56a1-95ce-759b9d4f6faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡z+\\pi -8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345818} {:answer/id #uuid "e8442034-9627-59f0-ad6b-e467f3306316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+2z-2y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "373d34ce-61bb-5a3a-a3a6-fef504beeec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345817} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b7ce3a-2397-54f6-a2d3-f73cacc91ccb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52af5bc1-756d-5cea-af80-685583e8af64" :question/math-academy-id "q-161566" :question/problem "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz\n$$\n\nis defined over the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-8\\le y\\le {sin}^{2}⁡z,\\,0\\le z\\le \\pi ,\\,-2y-1\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+2z}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/a905e1f60fb0aa865c071fcbccbfecb30dcac7a74b4b647d24a40f4c89ff1935.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=-2y-1,\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)={y}^{2}+2z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-8\\le y\\le {sin}^{2}⁡z,\\,0\\le z\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-8}^{{sin}^{2}⁡z}[{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dy\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-8}^{{sin}^{2}⁡z}{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=0+\\pi =\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd48398-cb9d-579f-b7f7-0f6be19172ce" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b298d70-c115-510f-9ceb-c9e0d66b55a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345822} {:answer/id #uuid "487a4a13-7ebc-57b2-b138-ce424a172816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345823} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c65824-061b-50e6-9559-071c9dbefca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345824} {:answer/id #uuid "9988de87-50d6-5cc5-8f3b-04a5d83b0c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345825} {:answer/id #uuid "b10f9425-6859-5bad-a114-f1a321d29e40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9988de87-50d6-5cc5-8f3b-04a5d83b0c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345825} :answer-field/id #uuid "36609a6b-13a3-5328-a8b9-7129fc23f252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d4d48da-93b4-5e0b-a47c-b8056a8a432d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161570" :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}4x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le y}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}4x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({(\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2{y}^{2}z+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(81-0) \\\\  & =54\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd48398-cb9d-579f-b7f7-0f6be19172ce" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327c7773-5dae-5bcf-8fa6-a2365f723a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345829} {:answer/id #uuid "b51e872d-153f-5642-9787-350a80175f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345830} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0314ee-3843-5e04-9478-b1f90d18a821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345831} {:answer/id #uuid "30f104d7-0280-507b-8db3-92abc8668141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345832} {:answer/id #uuid "0089b168-80b7-5fba-9e26-8af7e6bec32c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d0314ee-3843-5e04-9478-b1f90d18a821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345831} :answer-field/id #uuid "096fc5cf-f431-53fe-8d91-3eed2358a342" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dfdbc1e-41c1-5eba-82b0-7d445d5af9aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-161571" :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2z}}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,y\\le z\\le y+1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2z}}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{4}[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{2z}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{4}(2z-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{2}z\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{2}z\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{y+1}\\frac{1}{2}z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}[{z}^{2}]_{z=y}^{z=y+1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}((y+1)^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}(2y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[{y}^{2}+y]}_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(9+3)-\\frac{1}{4}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90adf237-9627-55f1-8f90-8fbc4acf02fc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29fd257e-187d-560a-8c07-c8cb5d56ce03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345836} {:answer/id #uuid "b9842096-2ee3-5bb7-9d74-ac9cd5d88db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-1}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345837} {:answer/id #uuid "dd842b71-aba2-5250-a4bd-b50a300b283b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345838} {:answer/id #uuid "525ec21c-bf14-5005-bcd9-ab9cb6c117d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-1}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345839} {:answer/id #uuid "933dc5e5-8d51-5602-b9f5-cdf0d13b16fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9842096-2ee3-5bb7-9d74-ac9cd5d88db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-1}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345837} :answer-field/id #uuid "52075f2e-7b6e-5241-b6d3-25133ec49afd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bde12b4-ffd2-5c0e-8555-b9fe3facf7ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-161575" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2+2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type II region given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,-z\\le x\\le 0}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2+2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-z}^{0}2+2{e}^{x}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-z}^{0}2+2{e}^{x}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-z}^{0}2+2{e}^{x}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[2x+2{e}^{x}]_{x=-z}^{x=0}dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}0+2{e}^{0}-2(-z)-2{e}^{-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2+2z-2{e}^{-z}\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2+2z-2{e}^{-z}\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}2+2z-2{e}^{-z}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[2z+{z}^{2}+2{e}^{-z}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2+1+2{e}^{-1})-2{e}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}1+2{e}^{-1}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}1+2{e}^{-1}\\,dy & =(1+2{e}^{-1})[y]_{y=-1}^{y=0} \\\\  & =(1+2{e}^{-1})(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90adf237-9627-55f1-8f90-8fbc4acf02fc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8365eb-0de9-505e-a99e-5929f21f44cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345843} {:answer/id #uuid "3dedf070-9163-5bfd-8414-8c71f4114c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345844} {:answer/id #uuid "8334554d-e6c0-5719-a407-6eb5431ecb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345845} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa5a7d7-33f6-5f78-b0ab-11f41d5afd8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345846} {:answer/id #uuid "58c7ff58-467d-5623-8910-3ba05fda5413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8365eb-0de9-505e-a99e-5929f21f44cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345843} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4a19db3-df16-55a0-ac05-56ac3de3c554" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42f45f71-27d5-5a4a-be80-b08c3b688e33" :question/math-academy-id "q-161581" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le y+z+2},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the $z$-axis and the line $z=2-y$ in the first quadrant." :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y+z+2}y\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y+z+2}y\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}y[x]_{x=0}^{x=y+z+2}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y(y+z+2)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}+yz+2y\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/b473cc75dbbd6fa36ba43f6bbdec4a0133164e6fd8bc5f76ff86cec74714a256.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}+yz+2y\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}{y}^{2}+yz+2y\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}{y}^{2}+yz+2y\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[({y}^{2}+2y)z+\\frac{1}{2}y{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2-y}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+2y)(2-y)+\\frac{1}{2}y(2-y)^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4y-{y}^{3})+\\frac{1}{2}y(4-4y+{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}+6y\\,dy \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =-2-\\frac{16}{3}+12 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90adf237-9627-55f1-8f90-8fbc4acf02fc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84f03db3-b90c-5c71-a84b-d3de3a197ccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345850} {:answer/id #uuid "4e843926-8cd5-5599-9b8b-9ad831c075b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345851} {:answer/id #uuid "b9cdf12f-b650-56de-b7f7-5d76c8840ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345852} {:answer/id #uuid "1b97ccb1-aa79-5691-9343-9914ab8a8e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345853} {:answer/id #uuid "9d71eca6-3e03-5d88-a34c-f94eeaa9a608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e843926-8cd5-5599-9b8b-9ad831c075b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345851} :answer-field/id #uuid "be55cf0f-6150-574d-9a8d-38e9c2d7d650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38ba2e71-6d19-5a55-bf3c-392d867b639c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161583" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2x\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le yz},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the line $y=1$ and the parabola $z={y}^{2}$ in the first quadrant." :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{yz}2x\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{yz}2x\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=yz}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(yz)^{2}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/acb173db9d28ed8b38e63c7bc365c20e5755aeb1af3f6340e7b9229a4b49101d.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}{z}^{3}]}_{z=0}^{z={y}^{2}}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}({y}^{6}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{8}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{9}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{9}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90adf237-9627-55f1-8f90-8fbc4acf02fc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31002da7-5aa8-5131-bed9-bfe5d9350bae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{72}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345857} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3c6399-6607-5c87-93e7-f06e701d6c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{34}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345858} {:answer/id #uuid "587e8c04-ddd1-5ee5-8403-ec1c90247522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{79}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345859} {:answer/id #uuid "2c023ca5-9eba-556b-996c-b06fca980833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{105}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345860} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc6af97-65f4-578e-b92a-db5b854954b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{92}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c023ca5-9eba-556b-996c-b06fca980833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{105}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345860} :answer-field/id #uuid "be53cb49-8a48-5762-aeaa-2ef555c03209" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "411a3ab9-0aa0-5668-8896-afd110dee3fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161585" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the line $y=1$ and the parabola $z=-{y}^{2}$ in the fourth quadrant." :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1}dx]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1}dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}[x]_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/38ea49a6fae41574107b2ba68a578586a306cf43d419d4de5699dcfa009137c2.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}}^{0}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}}^{0}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[({y}^{2}+1)z+\\frac{1}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=-{y}^{2}}^{z=0}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}+1)((0-(-{y}^{2}))+\\frac{1}{3}(0-{(-{y}^{2})}^{3})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}+1){y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{6}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{4}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{6}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{21}{y}^{7}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{105}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e612d4ba-b772-5f56-a031-fdfc05d63221" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "566221f6-05f9-5a62-ad5d-a14b8f6663c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345864} {:answer/id #uuid "4af2d5c0-c18a-5b00-bca0-f4d7f21685c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345865} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8d7283-92d8-5679-a77a-192d04ac0f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345866} {:answer/id #uuid "9b823093-1d5d-5b2c-81ca-cd77e7019fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345867} {:answer/id #uuid "23da081d-2cec-5f18-9eae-59476b85ed61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b823093-1d5d-5b2c-81ca-cd77e7019fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345867} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebd964e0-ee68-5278-b55d-92ee733d2366" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d80c8b49-f108-517b-a5d1-42a714175231" :question/math-academy-id "q-161589" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/d5690bff5819d72cc9d49016c119dc79f62f4d766c142a43a89df196ce9f2335.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/2427e053c0e2d808b32db5d7fedf81365e7e50f0b3f2dc61598ad1795394686e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2+2y-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/145f0d494ffe24de84e3a289b3a7316d3f4933f193d734aed118609f7fb9f94f.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2+2y-z}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2+2y-z}{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2+2y-z}{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2+2y-z}{y}^{2}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2+2y-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(2+2y-z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}z\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}z\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}z\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{y}^{2}z+2{y}^{3}z-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2})-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e612d4ba-b772-5f56-a031-fdfc05d63221" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f1afdc-c843-5ee1-bb6c-447b29d9f822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3yz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345871} {:answer/id #uuid "15b050f1-9e57-5b74-80e1-ff2e2422124e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345872} {:answer/id #uuid "15c5bfac-7a70-5074-b840-4cbd1d14a9d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345873} {:answer/id #uuid "137455a8-e5cd-540f-9859-24f40273b3fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345874} {:answer/id #uuid "d79a42bd-4ffe-5589-9366-db84c051c4e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}3z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15c5bfac-7a70-5074-b840-4cbd1d14a9d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345873} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c8e251d-48b1-5631-af6f-1b4cd3f362c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f7a494d-dd58-51ac-963c-d9b00a8df0cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161594" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/f1f3493cd1ff0bee115bf7c311504a2b8a9bbd38dd35adae96c9f7abcae40ae1.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type II region shown above, \n${∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "For a type II region $R$ with projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane, the triple integral of a continuous function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type II region enclosed between the back surface $x={u}_{1}(y,z)=1$ and the front surface $x={u}_{2}(y,z)=2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/6cc327f63d7141c43852e868812a7cff1ec9ae0acfed636ff896ceb204b6fbf0.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,1\\le x\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I *plane* region in the $yz$-plane with the upper curve\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\,⟺\\,z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/a0fb19d32c2545e5c2a61cbb54536e7c28b1a54dbce51c7752c3687035d6c3d6.png)\n\nHence, $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}[{x}^{2}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e612d4ba-b772-5f56-a031-fdfc05d63221" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81f5c9aa-ae0a-53ad-af70-47d8421072c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345878} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f7c3d4-0e9c-5b1a-b1d5-dd75f724d77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345879} {:answer/id #uuid "242096ed-d41a-5d6d-98e1-028871bfa249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345880} {:answer/id #uuid "83ff5443-55a6-5aac-82c7-b81c2cfd2eca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345881} {:answer/id #uuid "d26d4c23-36ab-5376-82dd-b1fed6968883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "242096ed-d41a-5d6d-98e1-028871bfa249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345880} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd620578-dd05-5846-bced-85b22a8b4888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61ab6bf1-137b-57a1-bf89-390949bf96b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161783" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/3fe3ec24f8f8006f22bde8ba2dc39fd3cd3f73668b06f8ea4a74e4ed5206a193.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/2a20f2651c24e48871028b44fe61da4600d632481877ef32ccdfd3241262a53b.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 5-y-\\frac{z}{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/1ba7c02f254313a73a813e4e43c44c25523ce77dd47e594a1db3b412a6bd5c3d.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 5-y-\\frac{z}{2}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{5-y-z/2}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{5-y-z/2}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{5-y-z/2}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}[x]_{x=0}^{x=5-y-z/2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}(5-y-\\frac{z}{2})\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{z}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=4}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2\\,dy & =[2y]_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e612d4ba-b772-5f56-a031-fdfc05d63221" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b00137a0-9917-53ee-8915-833087c8b564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345885} {:answer/id #uuid "c080851d-f994-5c21-b4d4-953fda191d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345886} {:answer/id #uuid "fb031b68-4086-5c4a-88f0-100aa7a5ef3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345887} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc96c16-507f-5e39-9e06-cbdeb1d8766d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345888} {:answer/id #uuid "0830b5d7-d144-5d72-a3e9-b81f570e22c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c080851d-f994-5c21-b4d4-953fda191d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345886} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8270bc6-063c-537c-bfa3-2431ffe6f033" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31e4325-59b1-526f-a289-64b327737018" :question/math-academy-id "q-161784" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/ca49b92d602d04a73dc7ab9936d601654bd293ce3fb7950372ac86155875aca3.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{6-y+3z}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/0008ba406cfe8a5b61d683a6854260897cb441752a5333add281ab1279e31b0b.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{y}{3}+z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/bbe6b6b897e5d53421080ae8e8b9dc49f2ea1b7e135f8426ee3f11af01cc2401.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le 2-\\frac{y}{3}+z}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{6-y+3z}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y/3+z}\\frac{1}{6-y+3z}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y/3+z}\\frac{1}{6-y+3z}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-y/3+z}\\frac{1}{6-y+3z}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6-y+3z}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2-y/3+z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6-y+3z}(2-\\frac{y}{3}+z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}{[z]}_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}(3)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,dy & =[y]_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90adf237-9627-55f1-8f90-8fbc4acf02fc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d488ebe0-9dde-539d-9806-021bc1b09cd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131601} {:answer/id #uuid "02d23ad9-b8b7-58fd-8c6f-449d308835c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131602} {:answer/id #uuid "0c90dde8-ae27-59ec-9c01-2274aa4d6671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131603} {:answer/id #uuid "36e1062b-0e17-5f5f-96c5-030f88ec8cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131604} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb1dd30-f4f1-55b7-acff-5b7c33f91bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d488ebe0-9dde-539d-9806-021bc1b09cd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131601} :answer-field/id #uuid "298c69f2-8ca2-54cb-980e-894b604ba719" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02ff2794-de30-53c1-a133-ec1abbd3f0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-40208" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2y\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le z\\sqrt{3}},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axes, the line $y=2$ and the line $z=y$ in the first quadrant." :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z\\sqrt{3}}2y\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z\\sqrt{3}}2y\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}2y[x]_{x=0}^{x=z\\sqrt{3}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2yz\\sqrt{3}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/63a8768b2769fad339ba7667357812d2dda0ea856e846ad666a100f8bc1336e9.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2yz\\sqrt{3}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2yz\\sqrt{3}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}2z\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{3}[{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=y}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{3}({y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{16}{4}-0) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e612d4ba-b772-5f56-a031-fdfc05d63221" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type II Region Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d07b9ef-58af-527d-847e-ae7482a3e790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131619} {:answer/id #uuid "a30e89de-0337-5ffc-9dad-b0b68f78df67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131620} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe90321-97df-58a9-8ef1-32b725cc649b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131621} {:answer/id #uuid "05034627-feaa-51bc-8ffa-542dfc4790d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131622} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1739f8-d406-5653-adf3-596aee499f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a30e89de-0337-5ffc-9dad-b0b68f78df67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131620} :answer-field/id #uuid "e99a475d-172e-5f35-8c7f-246a2fffd73f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1a1cf7f-cf9c-51a1-babc-d27035fc1ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-40219" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/bc1b0ea463b0f753a2c65784fa5d46203cde8f457116c481301bf3f20ce82f54.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}4y\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/9bb9311cbaf796f76c4b8b7cb7ed81abec48abc445b894f838f940fbd92e534b.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/82f237dc5fb7d8b8ed34b7a1c16527ab338923a33462f0bd7dafbe54bc5e5158.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y-z}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4y\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}4y\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}4y\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}4y\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4y[x]_{x=0}^{x=4-y-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4y(4-y-z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4{y}^{2}-4yz)\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4{y}^{2}-4yz)\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4{y}^{2}-4yz)\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[16yz-4{y}^{2}z-2y{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(14y-4{y}^{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(14y-4{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={[7{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =(28-\\frac{32}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd48398-cb9d-579f-b7f7-0f6be19172ce" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c163b1-1d8c-5353-8878-3b13afaff8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131703} {:answer/id #uuid "c331fd74-3fe7-5f07-aff0-9d0fccfed4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131704} {:answer/id #uuid "e96718c2-1174-5d23-a5bd-e18ba9605bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131705} {:answer/id #uuid "f80d2460-7c35-5d8f-be8c-aee3a6d918a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131706} {:answer/id #uuid "03136157-253b-5e52-a825-c65e603018ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03136157-253b-5e52-a825-c65e603018ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131707} :answer-field/id #uuid "da61fd60-b90c-54fb-a28f-baa9844cebb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df0fc1c3-daea-50ab-b3dc-d1aac4fefd9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40395" :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}12z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}12z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}12z[x]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12z(y-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12yz\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}yz\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{y}^{2}}12yz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6y[{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=4-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6y(4-{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(96y-48{y}^{3}+6{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[48{y}^{2}-12{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =48(2)^{2}-12(2)^{4}+(2)^{6} \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
